/* lore/en.lore.js — v613: descriptions for the "Описание созвездий" tab (core/lore.js), written for the site
   (own words, not copied from any book or wiki). Loaded only when the tab is opened. */
KC.addLore("en", {
  "dnd.al.CE": "Chaotic Evil is the alignment of those driven by cruelty, rage or a thirst for destruction. Such characters recognize no rules or limits, guided only by their own desires. It is the image of the mad destroyer or demon of the Abyss. They are the most unpredictable and dangerous adversaries.",
  "dnd.al.CG": "Chaotic Good is the alignment of those who do good guided by their own conscience rather than laws. Such characters value freedom, dislike rules and authority, and will break the law for a righteous cause. It is the image of the noble outlaw or rebel against tyranny. They are kind at heart but unpredictable.",
  "dnd.al.CN": "Chaotic Neutral is the alignment of those who value personal freedom above all. Such characters follow their own desires and whims, caring little for either laws or morality. They are not necessarily cruel, but they don't go out of their way to help others either. It is the image of the free-spirited wanderer or unpredictable adventurer.",
  "dnd.al.LE": "Lawful Evil is the alignment of those who pursue selfish or cruel goals within a strict order, hierarchy or code. Such characters use law as a tool of power and keep their word when it serves them. It is the image of the tyrant, scheming diplomat or devil striking bargains. They are dangerous for their calculation.",
  "dnd.al.LG": "Lawful Good is an alignment that combines a drive to do good with respect for law, order and one's given word. Such characters do the right thing because they believe in justice and honor, and they expect the same of others. It is the classic image of the noble knight, honest judge or devoted protector. They may struggle when law conflicts with morality.",
  "dnd.al.LN": "Lawful Neutral is the alignment of those who follow law, tradition or a personal code above all else, regardless of whether it is good or evil. Such characters value order, discipline and predictability. It is the image of the impartial judge, loyal soldier or monk living by strict rules. For them, the rule matters more than feelings.",
  "dnd.al.NE": "Neutral Evil is the alignment of those who put their own gain above everything, caring for neither order nor chaos. Such characters will do anything for their goals if they can get away with it. They don't necessarily seek destruction, but they feel no remorse either. It is the image of the unprincipled mercenary.",
  "dnd.al.NG": "Neutral Good is the alignment of those who do good without strong attachment to following or breaking rules. Such characters help others because it is right, choosing whatever path brings the most benefit. They may work with authority or around it. It is the image of the kind healer or selfless helper.",
  "dnd.al.NN": "True Neutral is the alignment of those who lean neither toward good nor evil, law nor chaos. Some such characters simply live their lives and avoid moral questions, while others deliberately seek balance among the world's forces. It is often the image of a druid preserving nature's balance. They act as circumstances require.",
  "dnd.c.artificer": "The Artificer is a maker who unites magic and craft, channeling spells through tools, devices and enchanted items. The class debuted in the Eberron setting, where magic is woven into technology, and later appeared in Tasha's Cauldron of Everything. Artificers create and improve magic items and share them with allies. They are inventive supporters and problem-solvers, at home both in battle and in the workshop.",
  "dnd.c.barbarian": "The Barbarian is a fierce warrior who draws strength from primal rage. While raging, they grow tougher, hit harder and barely notice their wounds. Barbarians often come from wild or frontier lands and trust instinct more than the rules of civilization. It is the classic front-line fighter who charges into the thick of battle and soaks up enemy blows.",
  "dnd.c.bard": "The Bard is a master of words, music and magic who believes the world resonates with a creative power that can be channeled. Bards inspire allies, confound enemies, and cast spells of enchantment, healing and illusion. They know a little about everything and connect easily with people. A Bard is a versatile support character, equally useful in negotiation, exploration and combat.",
  "dnd.c.cleric": "The Cleric is a servant of a deity or higher power, gifted with divine magic. Clerics heal the wounded, protect allies, turn undead and call down the wrath of the heavens upon foes. Much of a cleric's character comes from their domain, the god's sphere of influence, such as life, tempest, war or trickery. Clerics wear armor and can hold their own in a fight, combining the roles of healer and warrior.",
  "dnd.c.druid": "The Druid is a guardian of nature who draws power from the life of the world, the elements and ancient primal forces. Druids can take the shape of beasts, command plants, weather and the elements, and heal. They often live far from cities and protect the balance between civilization and the wild. The Druid is a flexible spellcaster able both to support allies and to fight in animal form.",
  "dnd.c.fighter": "The Fighter is a combat professional skilled with every weapon and armor. They might be a knight, mercenary, gladiator or war veteran, all united by honed skill and endurance. Fighters attack more often than others and can quickly recover in the middle of a battle. The class is easy to learn, yet its subclasses allow very different characters, from tacticians to battle mages.",
  "dnd.c.monk": "The Monk is a martial artist who fights with bare hands and simple weapons, channeling the inner energy of the body. Monks are incredibly fast, deftly evade attacks and perform feats that seem supernatural. Many trained in monasteries with their own traditions and philosophies. The Monk is a mobile combatant who unleashes flurries of blows and repositions swiftly.",
  "dnd.c.paladin": "The Paladin is a holy warrior whose power springs from a sacred oath. Paladins combine heavy armor and weapon mastery with divine magic: they heal, shield allies with auras and deliver devastating smites. The tenets of their oath shape their ideals, from knightly honor to relentless vengeance. A Paladin is a dependable front-line fighter with a strong moral stance.",
  "dnd.c.ranger": "The Ranger is a hunter and warden of the wilds who blends martial training with nature magic. Rangers track prey, survive in the wilderness and guide parties along dangerous routes. Many specialize in fighting particular threats, guarding the borders of civilization. A Ranger is a sharp-eyed archer or agile melee fighter, valuable in both scouting and battle.",
  "dnd.c.rogue": "The Rogue is a nimble specialist who relies on cunning, stealth and precision. Rogues pick locks, disarm traps, slip through shadows and strike with deadly accuracy when a foe is distracted. They might be thieves, spies, scouts or adventurers. Rogues master many skills and are especially valuable outside combat, while remaining dangerous in a fight.",
  "dnd.c.sorcerer": "The Sorcerer is a mage whose power is innate, flowing in their blood from a draconic heritage, a touch of chaos or another unusual source. Sorcerers don't learn spells from books; they feel magic as part of themselves. Their signature feature is metamagic, which lets them reshape spells by empowering, quickening or subtly casting them. A Sorcerer is a vivid spellcaster who knows fewer spells but wields them with virtuosity.",
  "dnd.c.warlock": "The Warlock is a spellcaster who gained power through a pact with a mighty otherworldly entity: a fiend, an archfey, a celestial, or something stranger still. Warlocks know fewer spells than other mages but regain their magic quickly and enhance themselves with eldritch invocations. The relationship with the patron is an endless source of story, from service and debt to doubt. A Warlock blends offensive magic with unusual abilities.",
  "dnd.c.wizard": "The Wizard is a scholarly mage who unlocks the secrets of magic through books, research and years of dedicated study. They keep their spells in a spellbook that can be expanded with new formulas. Wizards command the broadest arsenal of magic, from fireballs to teleportation and reality-altering spells. A Wizard is fragile but extraordinarily versatile, winning through knowledge and preparation.",
  "dnd.r.changeling": "Changelings are natural shapeshifters, able to alter their appearance, voice and features at will. The people originated in the world of Eberron but appear in other settings too. Many changelings keep several personas for different situations, and few ever see their true form, pale with colorless eyes. Their stories often explore identity, disguise and trust. Changelings make superb bards, rogues and warlocks.",
  "dnd.r.dragonborn": "Dragonborn are a people who carry the legacy of dragons: scales, a proud bearing and the ability to exhale elemental energy like their forebears. Scale color and breath type depend on which draconic line the character descends from, such as fire for red and gold or cold for white and silver. Dragonborn place great value on honor, self-improvement and loyalty to their clan. They shine as fighters, paladins and sorcerers.",
  "dnd.r.drow": "Drow are elves whose history is bound to the Underdark, the vast cavern world beneath the surface. They see in darkness better than most peoples, and many find sunlight unfamiliar. In classic settings such as the Forgotten Realms, drow societies are often portrayed as harsh matriarchies devoted to the spider goddess Lolth, yet many drow characters flee that heritage to forge their own path. In the 2024 rules, drow are one of the elven lineages, with innate magic of darkness.",
  "dnd.r.dwarf": "Dwarves are a sturdy, resilient people who have long dwelled in mountain strongholds and underground cities. They are renowned as smiths, miners and stonemasons, can see in the dark and shrug off poison well. Dwarves value clan, tradition and their given word, and they remember both kindness and grudges for a long time. They make dependable warriors and artisans, well suited to fighters, clerics and paladins.",
  "dnd.r.elf": "Elves are a long-lived people tied to magic and to the Feywild, where their ancestors are said to have originated. They are graceful, keen-sensed, and instead of ordinary sleep they enter a meditative trance. Living for centuries, they cherish art, nature and beauty, and often regard the affairs of shorter-lived peoples with gentle detachment. Elves are divided into several lineages, such as high elves and wood elves.",
  "dnd.r.goliath": "Goliaths are towering folk descended from giants. They live in harsh highlands, value fair competition and personal achievement, and respect those who prove themselves through deeds. Their powerful build lets them carry heavy loads and endure the cold, and in the newer rules they also inherit a spark of power from a particular kind of giant, such as fire, frost or stone giants. Goliaths are excellent barbarians, fighters and monks.",
  "dnd.r.halfling": "Halflings are a small but remarkably resilient people who treasure comfort, good food and close company. They are famous for almost uncanny luck and for keeping their nerve in danger, while their small size lets them slip through places others cannot. Many live peacefully in their communities, but some are called away by curiosity and the open road. Halflings make excellent rogues, bards and rangers.",
  "dnd.r.halforc": "Half-Orcs are descendants of humans and orcs, combining human versatility with orcish strength and endurance. They are known for sheer tenacity, able to keep standing in a crisis where others would fall. Many must find their place between two worlds and prove their worth to those around them. They are a classic choice for barbarians and fighters, though a half-orc can be anything. In the 2024 core rules, the Orc appears instead of the half-orc.",
  "dnd.r.human": "Humans are the most widespread people across the worlds of D&D. Their lives are short compared to elves or dwarves, which drives them to achieve as much as they can: they build empires, trade, explore and wage wars. They are known for adaptability, ambition and an enormous variety of cultures. A Human fits any class and any backstory, from a village shepherd to a court mage.",
  "dnd.r.kenku": "Kenku are a raven-like bird people known for perfectly mimicking any sound or voice. Legends say their ancestors once served a powerful patron and lost something precious, though in the newer rules kenku are no longer bound by that old curse. Kenku are observant, have excellent memories and skillfully reproduce what they have seen. They suit rogues, rangers and bards well.",
  "dnd.r.tabaxi": "Tabaxi are a feline people said to have come from distant lands. They are agile, nimble and swift, climb well and see in the dark, with claws that serve as both weapons and tools. Their defining trait is boundless curiosity: they collect stories, curiosities and knowledge, though they may jump quickly from one fascination to the next. Tabaxi feel natural as rogues, rangers, monks and bards.",
  "dnd.r.tiefling": "Tieflings are humans or other folk with the blood of the Lower Planes in their veins, visible in horns, tails, or unusual skin and eye colors. They possess innate magic and resilience tied to their origin; the 2024 rules describe infernal, abyssal and chthonic legacies. Because of their appearance they often face mistrust, so many tieflings rely on their own charm and determination. They make a fine choice for warlocks, sorcerers and bards.",
  "dnd.r.yuanti": "Yuan-ti are serpent folk whose ancestors fused their nature with that of snakes through ancient, dark rituals in pursuit of power. Many look nearly human but give themselves away with scales, serpentine eyes or a forked tongue. Yuan-ti resist poison and hostile magic and wield innate spells. Their societies are traditionally cold and calculating, yet a yuan-ti character may reject that legacy. They appear as a playable people in Monsters of the Multiverse.",
  "dnd.s.artificer.alchemist": "The Alchemist is an artificer subclass, a master of potions and chemical concoctions. They brew experimental elixirs with unpredictable beneficial effects and use their expertise to enhance healing and offensive magic. Alchemists support the party well by restoring allies' strength. The subclass suits lovers of laboratories and surprising results.",
  "dnd.s.artificer.armorer": "The Armorer is an artificer subclass that turns a suit of armor into a magical power suit. The armorer can tune the armor as a Guardian that draws enemy attention or as an Infiltrator built for stealth. Integrated weapons and upgrades make the Armorer a sturdy melee or ranged combatant. Introduced in Tasha's Cauldron of Everything, it blends craftsmanship with the image of a steel-clad hero.",
  "dnd.s.artificer.artillerist": "The Artillerist is an artificer subclass specializing in destructive magic and arcane weaponry. They craft an eldritch cannon that can spew flame, fire force bolts or protect allies. Their wands and staffs empower offensive spells. The subclass suits players who want to strike enemies powerfully from afar.",
  "dnd.s.artificer.battlesmith": "The Battle Smith is an artificer subclass that blends the roles of fighter, protector and craftsperson. Their key ally is the steel defender, a mechanical companion that fights at their side and shields friends. Battle Smiths apply their intellect to wielding weapons and can also mend allies. It suits players who want an inventor with a loyal mechanical friend.",
  "dnd.s.barbarian.beast": "The Path of the Beast is a barbarian subclass in which rage awakens a bestial nature. While raging, the barbarian grows fangs, claws or a tail and fights with them as natural weapons. These barbarians may have been touched by lycanthropy or another beast that left a mark on their blood. From Tasha's Cauldron of Everything, it suits fierce, feral characters.",
  "dnd.s.barbarian.berserker": "The Path of the Berserker is a barbarian subclass that turns rage into pure frenzy. Berserkers hurl themselves into battle with no thought for defense, attacking again and again while shrugging off fear and charm. Their very presence on the battlefield can terrify enemies. It is a straightforward, powerful path for those who want to play an unbridled warrior.",
  "dnd.s.barbarian.stormherald": "The Path of the Storm Herald is a barbarian subclass whose rage wraps them in an elemental storm. While raging, they emanate an aura reflecting their homeland: the heat of the desert, the power of the sea or the chill of the tundra. This aura harms enemies or protects allies. From Xanathar's Guide to Everything, it suits characters connected to the elements.",
  "dnd.s.barbarian.wildheart": "The Path of the Wild Heart is a barbarian subclass from the 2024 Player's Handbook, the successor to the earlier Path of the Totem Warrior. Its followers see animals as kin and draw on their spirits, such as the bear, eagle or wolf. Depending on the chosen beast, the barbarian becomes tougher, more mobile, or a boon to allies. It suits characters with a deep bond to the wild.",
  "dnd.s.bard.creation": "The College of Creation is a bard subclass that believes the world was made from a primordial song. These bards weave notes of creation into their magic, animating objects into dance and making items from nothing. Their inspiration grants allies extra benefits. From Tasha's Cauldron of Everything, it suits creative and inventive players.",
  "dnd.s.bard.dance": "The College of Dance is a bard subclass from the 2024 Player's Handbook that turns movement into both art and weapon. These bards fight without armor or weapons, relying on grace and rhythm as if dancing. Their inspiration helps allies move in swift harmony. The subclass blends support with nimble melee combat.",
  "dnd.s.bard.eloquence": "The College of Eloquence is a bard subclass devoted to persuasion and the art of oratory. These bards command words so well that their arguments are nearly impossible to refute, and their inspiration is especially reliable. They can both uplift allies and undermine enemies' confidence. The subclass suits diplomats, orators and charismatic leaders.",
  "dnd.s.bard.glamour": "The College of Glamour is a bard subclass that draws inspiration from the magic of the Feywild. These bards master the magic of entrancing beauty and majesty, able to captivate listeners or inspire awe and dread. Their performances embolden allies, and they themselves seem wreathed in radiance. From Xanathar's Guide to Everything, it suits charismatic, mysterious characters.",
  "dnd.s.cleric.life": "The Life Domain is a cleric subclass devoted to gods of life, vitality and healing. These clerics are the finest healers of any class: their restorative magic is especially potent, and they can wear heavy armor. They protect the weak, fight disease and undeath, and embody the force that sustains all living things. It is the classic choice for a player who wants to keep the party on its feet.",
  "dnd.s.cleric.tempest": "The Tempest Domain is a cleric subclass serving gods of thunder, lightning and the sea. These clerics wear heavy armor and unleash the power of storms on their foes through thunderclaps and lightning strikes. They embody the fury of nature and the might of the heavens. The subclass suits warlike clerics who prefer offense to defense.",
  "dnd.s.cleric.trickery": "The Trickery Domain is a cleric subclass serving gods of mischief, cunning and change. These clerics wield illusion, invisibility and duplicates that confuse enemies. They challenge established orders and tyrants, preferring guile to open confrontation. The subclass suits slippery, playful and unpredictable characters.",
  "dnd.s.druid.dreams": "The Circle of Dreams is a druid subclass tied to the Feywild and its enchantment. These druids carry a spark of the fey's dreamlike, restorative power: they heal allies, guard restful camps and can step through moonlight and shadow. It is a gentle, supportive path for those who see nature as a source of hope as well as wildness. The subclass appears in Xanathar's Guide to Everything.",
  "dnd.s.druid.land": "The Circle of the Land is a druid subclass tied to a particular landscape, such as desert, forest, mountain or swamp. These druids are keepers of ancient lore and mystic rites, and their magic reflects the nature of their home region. They rely more on spells than on beast forms. It is the classic choice for a wise nature spellcaster.",
  "dnd.s.druid.moon": "The Circle of the Moon is a druid subclass devoted to the art of transforming into beasts. These druids assume mightier animal forms than others and fight in them on the front line. They live at the wildest edges of nature, attuned to the rhythm of the moon. The subclass suits players who want to play a shapeshifting protector.",
  "dnd.s.druid.sea": "The Circle of the Sea is a druid subclass from the 2024 Player's Handbook, tied to the power of the ocean and storms. Its followers call forth the wrath of the sea, surrounding themselves with icy spray and wind that harm and push away nearby foes. They feel the tides and currents as part of themselves and eventually move through water with ease. It suits players who want a formidable elemental spellcaster.",
  "dnd.s.fighter.battlemaster": "The Battle Master is a fighter subclass that turns combat into a science. These warriors know special maneuvers such as trips, feints, disarms and commands to allies. They study their opponents and pick the right maneuver at the right moment. It suits players who love tactics and want more options in battle than a simple attack.",
  "dnd.s.fighter.champion": "The Champion is a fighter subclass that relies on pure physical excellence. Champions land critical hits more often, are notably resilient and athletic, and grow more formidable with each level. They don't need complex maneuvers; their strength lies in simplicity and reliability. It is an excellent choice for newcomers and those who want to play an unstoppable warrior.",
  "dnd.s.fighter.runeknight": "The Rune Knight is a fighter subclass that has mastered the ancient magic of giant runes. By inscribing runes on their gear, they gain special powers and can grow to giant size for a time. Each rune is tied to a kind of giant, such as fire, frost or cloud giants. From Tasha's Cauldron of Everything, it suits those who want a warrior's might with a touch of magic.",
  "dnd.s.monk.astral": "The Way of the Astral Self is a monk subclass able to manifest their soul as a ghostly astral form. At first the monk summons spectral arms that strike at reach, and later a fuller spiritual body. Followers of this path believe the body is an illusion and that true strength lies in the spirit. The subclass appears in Tasha's Cauldron of Everything.",
  "dnd.s.monk.mercy": "Warrior of Mercy is a monk subclass whose followers master the art of both healing and taking life. They wear masks that hide their identity and wander, aiding the suffering and ending torment. With a single touch, such a monk can mend an ally's wounds or harm a foe. It began in Tasha's Cauldron of Everything as the Way of Mercy and joined the core rules in 2024.",
  "dnd.s.monk.openhand": "Warrior of the Open Hand is a monk subclass that perfects pure unarmed mastery. Its followers learn to knock foes down, push them away and deny them reactions, turning every strike into a tool of control. At the height of the path, the monk can deal devastating harm with a single touch. It is a simple, dependable choice for anyone who wants to feel like a martial arts master.",
  "dnd.s.paladin.conquest": "The Oath of Conquest is a paladin subclass whose followers seek to impose order by force and crush chaos. These paladins strike terror into their foes, breaking their will and binding them with fear. Their ideals are authority, discipline and victory at any cost, so they often walk the line between stern law and tyranny. From Xanathar's Guide to Everything, the subclass suits commanding, menacing characters.",
  "dnd.s.paladin.devotion": "The Oath of Devotion is a paladin subclass that embodies the classic knight in shining armor. Its followers swear to be honest, brave and compassionate and to protect the weak. Their divine power sanctifies their weapons, wards off evil and shields allies from charm. It is the perfect choice for a noble protector who lives by lofty ideals.",
  "dnd.s.paladin.glory": "The Oath of Glory is a paladin subclass that strives for heroic deeds worthy of legend. These paladins are inspired by mythic heroes and hone body and spirit for great achievements. Their power makes them swift and athletic and emboldens their allies. The subclass suits heroes of epic sagas.",
  "dnd.s.paladin.vengeance": "The Oath of Vengeance is a paladin subclass sworn to punish those who have committed grievous evil. These paladins put punishing the guilty above their own purity and will go to great lengths for justice. Their power helps them hunt down and pursue a chosen target. The subclass suits grim, determined characters with a personal mission.",
  "dnd.s.ranger.beastmaster": "The Beast Master is a ranger subclass bonded with a loyal animal companion. The beast fights alongside its master, follows commands and grows stronger along with them. In newer rules, the companion embodies a primal force of nature and can take the form of a land, sea or sky beast. It is ideal for players who dream of a faithful creature at their side.",
  "dnd.s.ranger.gloomstalker": "The Gloom Stalker is a ranger subclass at home in darkness. These rangers hunt in dungeons and deep shadows, becoming nearly invisible to those relying on darkvision. They act swiftly at the start of combat, striking before their enemies can react. The subclass suits stealthy, deadly hunters.",
  "dnd.s.ranger.hunter": "The Hunter is a ranger subclass devoted to protecting the world from monsters and other dangers. Hunters choose techniques suited to particular threats, whether lone giants, hordes of foes or slippery opponents. They are cool-headed, observant and focused on efficiency. It is a flexible, straightforward path for a guardian of the frontier.",
  "dnd.s.rogue.arcanetrickster": "The Arcane Trickster is a rogue subclass that enhances thieving skills with wizard magic. These rogues use illusions and enchantments, and their invisible mage hand helps them steal and distract from afar. It is a crafty jack-of-all-trades who blends stealth with magical tricks. It suits mischievous and inventive characters.",
  "dnd.s.rogue.inquisitive": "The Inquisitive is a rogue subclass, a master of investigation, observation and exposing lies. These rogues notice the smallest details, read people and see through deception and disguise. In combat, they use their insight to find an opponent's weak points. From Xanathar's Guide to Everything, it is ideal for detective and intrigue campaigns.",
  "dnd.s.rogue.swashbuckler": "The Swashbuckler is a rogue subclass, a master of elegant swordplay and daring flair. These rogues excel in one-on-one duels, dart away after attacking and rely on charm and bravado. They don't need allies nearby to exploit an opponent's weakness. From Xanathar's Guide to Everything, it is ideal for pirate-style characters and romantic adventurers.",
  "dnd.s.sorcerer.aberrant": "Aberrant Sorcery is a sorcerer subclass whose power comes from contact with alien forces of the Far Realm or creatures like mind flayers. These sorcerers wield telepathy and psionic magic, and their bodies can take on strange traits. Their stories explore otherness and inner struggle. It builds on the Aberrant Mind from Tasha's Cauldron of Everything.",
  "dnd.s.sorcerer.draconic": "Draconic Sorcery is a sorcerer subclass whose magic is inherited from a dragon ancestor. Over time, these sorcerers develop scales, sturdiness and even wings, and spells of their ancestral element become especially powerful. They are proud and majestic like their forebears. The subclass suits vivid, commanding mages.",
  "dnd.s.sorcerer.storm": "Storm Sorcery is a sorcerer subclass whose power springs from wind and tempest. These sorcerers are surrounded by gusts, move easily through the air and command thunder and lightning. Many are tied to the sea or the Elemental Plane of Air. From Xanathar's Guide to Everything, it suits energetic, untamed characters.",
  "dnd.s.sorcerer.wildmagic": "Wild Magic Sorcery is a sorcerer subclass whose power stems from chaos and is unpredictable. Casting spells can trigger unexpected surges, ranging from harmless to destructive. They struggle to fully control their magic but can bend luck in their favor. The subclass suits players who love surprises.",
  "dnd.s.warlock.archfey": "The Archfey Patron is a warlock subclass whose pact is with a mighty being of the Feywild. Archfey are capricious, unpredictable and live by their own strange laws. The pact grants magic of charm, illusion and instant teleportation. The subclass suits mysterious, playful and charming characters.",
  "dnd.s.warlock.celestial": "The Celestial Patron is a warlock subclass whose pact is with a being of the Upper Planes, such as an angel, unicorn or other radiant entity. A warlock bound to such a patron gains magic of light and healing, unusual for the class. These warlocks often serve a greater good, though their patron's aims are not always clear to mortals. The subclass blends ally support with radiant offensive magic.",
  "dnd.s.warlock.fiend": "The Fiend Patron is a warlock subclass whose pact is with a being of the Lower Planes, such as a demon, devil or other dark power. The pact grants destructive fire magic and resilience, but it usually comes at a price. These warlocks often balance gain against danger, trying to wield power without losing themselves. The subclass suits dramatic stories of bargains and temptation.",
  "dnd.s.warlock.greatoldone": "The Great Old One Patron is a warlock subclass whose pact is with an unknowable entity from the distant stars or the depths of the Far Realm. Its motives are alien to mortals, and it may not even be aware of its warlock. The pact grants telepathy, power over minds and unsettling magic. Inspired by cosmic horror, the subclass is ideal for dark, enigmatic characters.",
  "dnd.s.wizard.enchantment": "The School of Enchantment is a wizard subclass that studies magic affecting the mind. Enchanters can inspire friendliness, bend wills and confuse enemies by redirecting their attention. This magic is especially valuable in negotiations and intrigue, but in combat it can also take foes out of the fight without bloodshed. It suits charming and calculating characters.",
  "dnd.s.wizard.illusion": "The Illusionist is a wizard subclass devoted to magic that deceives the senses. Illusionists craft false images, sounds and mirages, and over time learn to make their illusions nearly real. In clever hands this magic distracts enemies, hides allies and solves problems without a fight. The subclass rewards creativity and suits players who love unconventional solutions.",
  "dnd.s.wizard.transmutation": "The School of Transmutation is a wizard subclass that studies the alteration of matter and energy. Transmuters turn one substance into another, change the shapes of creatures and craft a transmuter's stone that grants special properties. They are fascinated by alchemy and the very nature of change. It suits curious characters who see the world as material for experiments.",
  "leg.l.al": "The Alpha Legion is the Twentieth Legion, the last to be created. Its primarchs are the twins Alpharius and Omegon, who long hid the fact that there were two of them. The Legion excels at espionage, deception, double agents and cults, and its warriors look identical and all call themselves 'Alpharius'. Why it sided with Horus remains a mystery, and its true goals are unknown. Its emblem is the many-headed hydra.",
  "leg.l.ba": "The Blood Angels are the Ninth Legion. Their primarch, the winged and beautiful Sanguinius, was perhaps the most beloved of the Emperor's sons. He fell in single combat with Horus aboard the Warmaster's flagship, but his sacrifice helped the Emperor prevail. The Legion carries a curse in its gene-seed, the Red Thirst and the Black Rage, a legacy of its primarch's death. The Blood Angels are noble, cultured warriors forever struggling with the darkness within.",
  "leg.l.da": "The Dark Angels are the First Legion, the earliest created by the Emperor. Their primarch, Lion El'Jonson, grew up hunting monsters in the forests of Caliban and became a stern, reserved knight-commander. The Legion was famed for discipline and access to the most dangerous weapons. It stayed loyal in the Heresy, but part of it rebelled on Caliban, becoming the Fallen. Ever since, the Dark Angels have secretly hunted them to hide their shame, and they are a Legion steeped in secrets.",
  "leg.l.dg": "The Death Guard are the Fourteenth Legion. Their primarch, Mortarion, grew up on the poisonous world of Barbarus under a sorcerous tyrant, against whom he rebelled. He hated psykers and sorcery, and his Legion was famed for unbreakable endurance. During the Heresy the Death Guard fleet was struck by a plague in the warp, and to save himself and his sons Mortarion gave himself to Nurgle. They are now bloated, rotting but nearly unkillable warriors of the Plague God.",
  "leg.l.ec": "The Emperor's Children are the Third Legion, the only one granted the right to bear the Emperor's aquila on their armour. Their primarch, Fulgrim, raised on the world of Chemos, was obsessed with perfection in all things. That pride opened the way to corruption: through a cursed blade Fulgrim fell under the sway of Slaanesh, betrayed the Emperor and slew his brother Ferrus Manus. The Legion degenerated into devotees of excess, forever seeking new sensations.",
  "leg.l.if": "The Imperial Fists are the Seventh Legion, unrivalled masters of defence. Their primarch, Rogal Dorn, was an unbending, honest and stubborn strategist whom the Emperor tasked with fortifying Terra. During the Horus Heresy, Dorn commanded the defence of the Imperial Palace. The Fists are known for endurance, pride and an inability to retreat. After the Heresy the Legion split into Chapters including the Black Templars and Crimson Fists.",
  "leg.l.ih": "The Iron Hands are the Tenth Legion. Their primarch, Ferrus Manus, raised on the harsh world of Medusa, had hands sheathed in living metal and despised the weakness of flesh. The Legion reveres machines and replaces its bodies with augmetics, sharing close ties with the Mechanicum. Ferrus was slain by his brother Fulgrim in the Isstvan V massacre, a devastating blow to the Legion. Since then the Iron Hands have grown even colder and more ruthless.",
  "leg.l.iw": "The Iron Warriors are the Fourth Legion, masters of siege warfare. Their primarch, Perturabo, raised on Olympia, was a brilliant engineer and general who felt undervalued, constantly given the hardest and most thankless sieges. Resentment and envy of Rogal Dorn led him to join Horus. The Iron Warriors are not bound to a single Chaos god but value persistence, calculation and firepower. They are ruthless and vengeful.",
  "leg.l.nl": "The Night Lords are the Eighth Legion, wielding terror as their chief weapon. Their primarch, Konrad Curze, grew up on the lightless, crime-ridden world of Nostramo, where he imposed order alone through fear, tormented by prophetic visions. The Legion cowed whole worlds into obedience. Having betrayed the Imperium, the Night Lords serve no Chaos god: they are raiders and tormentors driven by cruelty and spite. Curze was killed by an Imperial assassin, just as he had foreseen.",
  "leg.l.rg": "The Raven Guard are the Nineteenth Legion, masters of stealth, ambush and sudden strikes. Their primarch, Corvus Corax, grew up in a prison-mine on the moon Lycaeus and led the inmates' uprising, after which the moon was renamed Deliverance. He hated tyranny and always fought for the oppressed. The Legion was nearly wiped out at Isstvan V, and Corax went to desperate lengths to rebuild it. Raven Guard are quiet, reserved shadow-warriors.",
  "leg.l.sal": "The Salamanders are the Eighteenth Legion. Their primarch, Vulkan, grew up on the volcanic world of Nocturne and was a master smith and a protector of ordinary people. The Legion is marked by coal-black skin and red eyes, a love of flamers and meltas, and great craftsmanship. The Salamanders are known for their humanity, valuing the lives of civilians above glory. They suffered terribly at Isstvan V but survived and remained loyal.",
  "leg.l.soh": "The Sons of Horus are the Sixteenth Legion, originally named the Luna Wolves. Their primarch, Horus Lupercal, raised on the hive world Cthonia, was the Emperor's favoured son and was named Warmaster. But, struck down by a wound and deceived by the powers of Chaos, Horus raised the rebellion that tore the Imperium apart: the Horus Heresy. He fell in single combat with the Emperor aboard his flagship. The Legion was later reborn as the Black Legion under Abaddon the Despoiler.",
  "leg.l.sw": "The Space Wolves are the Sixth Legion. Their primarch, Leman Russ, was raised among the people of the ice world Fenris and became a fierce, blunt warrior-king. Known as the Wolves of Fenris, the Legion served as the Emperor's executioners for the harshest tasks, and it was they who fell upon Prospero, homeworld of the Thousand Sons. They spurn many conventions of the Codex, keeping a wild culture of sagas, runes and feasting, yet remain utterly loyal to the Imperium.",
  "leg.l.ts": "The Thousand Sons are the Fifteenth Legion, a Legion of sorcerers. Their primarch, Magnus the Red, was a one-eyed giant and the mightiest of psykers, who pursued knowledge on his homeworld Prospero. Trying to warn the Emperor of Horus's treachery, Magnus shattered Terra's psychic defences with sorcery. The Emperor sent the Space Wolves, and Prospero burned. In despair Magnus turned to Tzeentch. Later the sorcerer Ahriman's Rubric reduced most of the Legion to dust sealed inside their armour.",
  "leg.l.um": "The Ultramarines are the Thirteenth Legion, the largest at the time of the Horus Heresy. Their primarch, Roboute Guilliman, was a level-headed ruler and brilliant organiser who built the thriving realm of Ultramar around Macragge. After the Heresy he wrote the Codex Astartes, which split the Legions into Chapters. The Ultramarines became the model for all Space Marines, and Guilliman, revived after ten thousand years, once again leads the Imperium.",
  "leg.l.wb": "The Word Bearers are the Seventeenth Legion. Their primarch, Lorgar, raised on the theocratic world of Colchis, was deeply religious and worshipped the Emperor as a god. The Emperor publicly rebuked him for it, forcing the Legion to kneel in the ashes of the razed city of Monarchia. Humiliated, Lorgar found new gods in the powers of Chaos and became one of the chief architects of the Heresy. The Word Bearers are fanatical preachers of Chaos Undivided.",
  "leg.l.we": "The World Eaters are the Twelfth Legion. Their primarch, Angron, was a gladiator-slave on Nuceria, where he was fitted with the Butcher's Nails, an implant that drives uncontrollable rage and steals all joy. The Emperor took him away, leaving his comrades to die, and Angron never forgave it. The Legion adopted the implants and became a horde of berserkers. They joined the traitors in the Horus Heresy, later became servants of Khorne, and Angron ascended as a daemon prince.",
  "leg.l.ws": "The White Scars are the Fifth Legion, masters of lightning warfare. Their primarch, Jaghatai Khan, was raised among the nomadic tribes of the steppe world Chogoris and gave the Legion a culture of riders, honour and brotherhood. They favour swift raids on bikes and skimmers. During the Horus Heresy they wavered but stayed loyal and fought their way to defend Terra. Often underestimated as 'barbarians', they are in fact refined and proud warriors.",
  "leg.lost": "The Second and Eleventh are the two lost Legions, whose records were completely erased from Imperial archives even before the Horus Heresy. Neither their names, nor their primarchs' names, nor their fate are known. Even the other primarchs avoid speaking of them. Many rumours and theories exist, from betrayal to failed experiments to destruction in battle, but Games Workshop deliberately keeps the mystery unsolved.",
  "ow.grudges": "The Book of Grudges is the Dwarfs' greatest chronicle, recording every insult, betrayal and debt ever done to them. The Great Book of Grudges is kept by the High King in Karaz-a-Karak, while individual holds and clans keep their own. A grudge is only settled once fitting vengeance or recompense has been taken, and only then is it struck out. Many entries are over a thousand years old and concern elves, greenskins and Skaven. To the Dwarfs it is a matter of honour, not merely a memory of the past.",
  "ow.r.beast": "The Beastmen are horned children of Chaos, half-man and half-beast, who dwell in the deep forests of the Old World. They hate civilisation and seek to destroy everything humans have built, burning villages and defiling shrines. Their warherds are led by mighty chieftains, while shamans read the will of the dark gods at their herdstones. The Beastmen embody the primal savagery and bestial fury of Chaos.",
  "ow.r.bret": "Bretonnia is the chivalric kingdom of the Old World, founded on ideals of honour, courtliness and devotion to the Lady of the Lake. Its strength lies in its noble knights, from Knights Errant to the Grail Knights who have drunk from the holy chalice. The Prophetesses and Damsels guard the Lady's mysteries. For all the splendour of its knighthood, its peasants live in poverty and servitude, and harsh inequality lies beneath the gleam of honour.",
  "ow.r.cathay": "Cathay is the great empire of the Far East, first portrayed in depth in Total War: Warhammer III. It is ruled by the Celestial Dragon Emperor and the Moon Empress, and their dragon children, who take human form, lead its armies. Its society rests on the harmony of yin and yang, earthly strength and celestial magic. Cathay guards the Great Bastion, a colossal wall against Chaos, and fields jade warriors, Celestial Dragon Guard and war lanterns.",
  "ow.r.cd": "The Chaos Dwarfs are a branch of the dwarf race that fell into isolation in the Dark Lands and worship the bull-god Hashut. Their capital, Zharr-Naggrund, is a vast ziggurat-forge amid a volcanic wasteland. They are ruthless engineers and daemonsmiths who build daemonic war machines. Their industry runs on countless slaves, and over time their sorcerers literally turn to stone from their own magic.",
  "ow.r.de": "The Dark Elves (Druchii) are elves who broke away from their kin after the Sundering and fled to cold Naggaroth. They are ruled by the Witch King Malekith, who believes himself the rightful lord of Ulthuan, and his mother Morathi. Their society is built on cruelty, slavery and the worship of Khaine, god of murder. They are infamous for raids from their Black Arks, their Witch Elves and their treachery. Their eternal aim is vengeance upon the High Elves.",
  "ow.r.dwarf": "The Dwarfs are an ancient, proud and stubborn people who live in mountain holds beneath the Worlds Edge Mountains. Their empire once flourished, but wars, earthquakes and invasions by greenskins and Skaven have stripped them of much of it. Dwarfs are peerless smiths, engineers and runesmiths who revere their ancestors and value gold, ale and a promise kept. They never forget a wrong and record every one in the Book of Grudges.",
  "ow.r.emp": "The Empire is the largest human realm of the Old World, founded by Sigmar, the chieftain who united the tribes of men and was later worshipped as a god. It is a confederation of provinces under an elected Emperor, centred on Altdorf. It relies on state troops, knightly orders, gunpowder weapons, steam tanks and wizards of the Colleges of Magic. Riven by politics and threatened on every side, it remains mankind's chief bulwark against Chaos.",
  "ow.r.he": "The High Elves are an ancient and proud race from the island of Ulthuan, where their ancestors created the Great Vortex that drains away the magic of Chaos. They are ruled by the Phoenix King, and their armies are known for disciplined infantry, mages and dragon riders. They wage an endless war against their sundered dark kin and have not forgotten their ancient feud with the Dwarfs. Often seen as arrogant, they remain among the world's foremost defenders.",
  "ow.r.kislev": "Kislev is a harsh northern realm that is the first to face the Chaos hordes from the Wastes. In Total War: Warhammer III it is ruled by the Ice Queen, Tzarina Katarin, a powerful ice witch, while Patriarch Kostaltyn leads the zealous faithful of the Great Orthodoxy. Kislevites worship the gods Ursun, Dazh and Tor, and their armies combine Kossar infantry, Winged Lancers, war bears and ice witches. They are a people of endurance, faith and grim survival.",
  "ow.r.liz": "The Lizardmen are an ancient race from the jungles of Lustria, created by the Old Ones to carry out their Great Plan. They are ruled by the wise, toad-like Slann mage-priests who wield immense magic. They are served by saurus warriors, agile skinks and huge reptiles such as stegadons. The Lizardmen are among the oldest foes of Chaos, though in pursuit of their plan they can be coldly indifferent to the fate of other peoples.",
  "ow.r.ogre": "Ogres are huge, ravenous creatures from the Ogre Kingdoms in the Mountains of Mourn. Their lives revolve around food: they worship the Great Maw and will eat almost anything. Ogres happily hire themselves out as mercenaries to any race that pays and feeds them, and their leaders are tyrants, the biggest and strongest of all. Crude and simple-minded, they are nevertheless cunning and formidable in battle, crushing foes with sheer bulk.",
  "ow.r.orc": "Orcs & Goblins, the greenskins, are the most numerous and restless foes of civilisation in the Old World. Orcs are big, strong and live for fighting; goblins are small, cowardly and sneaky, but no less dangerous in a mob. From time to time a great warboss unites the tribes into a Waaagh! and hurls them at their neighbours. Driven by a love of battle and loot rather than grand plans, they are unpredictable, comical and deadly.",
  "ow.r.skaven": "The Skaven are a race of intelligent ratmen living in a vast network of tunnels beneath the Old World, the Under-Empire. Their capital is Skavenblight, and power rests with the Council of Thirteen. They are immensely numerous, cowardly and treacherous, forever scheming against one another. Using warpstone, they build insane inventions and spread plague. Most humans believe they are merely a myth.",
  "ow.r.tk": "The Tomb Kings are the ancient rulers of Nehekhara, a land reminiscent of ancient Egypt. Their civilisation died when the great necromancer Nagash performed a ritual that raised the dead of the whole land. Awakened, the Tomb Kings found the promised paradise was a lie, and now rule armies of skeletons, animated statues and chariots. They are haughty, bound to ancient traditions and hate Nagash and the vampires.",
  "ow.r.vamp": "The Vampires, or Vampire Counts, are the immortal undead lords of Sylvania and other dark lands of the Old World. Their bloodline traces back to Neferata and her followers in ancient Lahmia. They command armies of skeletons, zombies and spirits raised by necromancy. The most famous are the von Carstein dynasty, who once tried to conquer the Empire. Aristocratic, cruel and forever hungry, they see the living only as cattle and subjects.",
  "ow.r.we": "The Wood Elves are descended from the elves who stayed in the Old World when their kin withdrew to Ulthuan. They live in the enchanted forest of Athel Loren in alliance with its tree spirits, ruled jointly by Queen Ariel and King Orion. Masters of the bow and the ambush, they defend their forest fiercely against all intruders. They have little love for outsiders, and their concerns centre on preserving the balance of nature.",
  "ow.r.woc": "The Warriors of Chaos are the chosen mortals of the northern Chaos Wastes who have devoted themselves to the dark gods. Clad in heavy armour, they seek glory and divine favour, hoping one day to become daemon princes. They are led by mighty lords such as Archaon the Everchosen, followed by Northmen tribes and daemons. Time and again they surge south, threatening to destroy the entire Old World.",
  "wh.f.aeldari": "The Aeldari are an ancient, refined and proud race that once ruled the galaxy. Their empire fell when their excesses gave birth to Slaanesh, and they have been a dwindling people ever since. Most live on vast craftworlds, keeping themselves in check through the disciplined Paths, while spirit stones protect their souls. They are gifted psykers and warriors with advanced technology, but they are few and every loss is irreplaceable. Guided by their Farseers, they often act in mysterious and arrogant ways.",
  "wh.f.csm": "Chaos Space Marines are Space Marines who betrayed the Emperor and gave themselves to the powers of Chaos. Their core is the nine Traitor Legions that rebelled in the Horus Heresy and fled into the Eye of Terror, joined by later renegades. For ten thousand years they have waged the Long War against the Imperium, driven by hatred, vengeance and the gifts of the dark gods. Many are veterans who personally remember the age of the Great Crusade.",
  "wh.f.dmech": "The Dark Mechanicum are renegade tech-priests who rejected the dogmas of Mars and turned to Chaos. The schism began in the Horus Heresy, when part of the Mechanicum sided with the traitors. Free of all restraint, dark magi experiment with forbidden technology, fusing machinery with flesh and daemons. They build daemon engines and serve the Traitor Legions, especially the Iron Warriors. To the Imperium they represent technology run wild.",
  "wh.f.drukhari": "The Drukhari are descended from those Aeldari who refused to give up their excesses even after the Fall. They live in Commorragh, a vast city hidden in the webway, sheltered from the warp. To keep Slaanesh from devouring their souls they sustain themselves on the suffering of others, raiding other worlds for captives and slaves. Their society is built on intrigue, cruelty and power struggles between kabals, wych cults and haemonculi.",
  "wh.f.gk": "The Grey Knights are a secret Space Marine Chapter created for a single purpose: the destruction of daemons. Every one of them is a psyker whose soul is tempered to resist the corruption of the warp. Based on the hidden moon of Titan, they work closely with the Inquisition's Ordo Malleus. Silver armour, blessed weapons and protective rites make them the Imperium's deadliest weapon against Chaos, and very few even know they exist.",
  "wh.f.harlequins": "The Harlequins are enigmatic wandering Aeldari who serve Cegorach, the Laughing God, the only Aeldari deity to survive the birth of Slaanesh. They travel the webway performing sacred dance-dramas that retell the history and myths of their people. Welcome on craftworlds and in Commorragh alike, they bind together the scattered branches of their race. In battle they are blindingly fast, acrobatic warriors in bright masks, and they guard the secrets of the Black Library.",
  "wh.f.ig": "The Imperial Guard (Astra Militarum) is the vast army of the Imperium, made up of billions of ordinary soldiers raised from countless worlds. Its strength lies not in training or equipment but in sheer numbers, tanks, artillery and a willingness to hold the line to the last. Lasguns, Leman Russ tanks and commissars who execute cowards are its icons. Guardsmen are ordinary people facing the nightmares of the galaxy, and their endurance is one of the Imperium's main pillars.",
  "wh.f.mech": "The Adeptus Mechanicus is the priesthood of the Machine God, centred on Mars, and the keeper of the Imperium's technology. Tech-priests revere machines as vessels of spirit and worship the Omnissiah, whom they identify with the Emperor. They gradually replace their flesh with augmetics, for 'the flesh is weak'. They build the weapons, starships and Titans of the Imperium, but would rather recover lost ancient knowledge than invent anew. The pact between Mars and Terra underpins the Imperium itself.",
  "wh.f.necrons": "The Necrons are an ancient race that were once the mortal Necrontyr. Seeking immortality and victory over the Old Ones, they surrendered their bodies to the star gods, the C'tan, and became beings of living metal. For sixty million years they slept in their tomb worlds, and now they are awakening to reclaim the galaxy. Most Necrons lost their minds, but their nobility kept their personalities, ambitions and eccentricities. They are extremely hard to destroy, as fallen warriors repair themselves or phase away.",
  "wh.f.orks": "Orks are a green-skinned warrior race spread across the entire galaxy. They live for fighting and relish any war, and size and strength decide who is in charge. Orks reproduce by spores, shrug off terrible injuries and build weapons and vehicles from scrap that somehow work because they believe they will. Their collective psychic energy grows stronger as they gather in numbers. For all their comic appearance, they are one of the gravest threats in the galaxy.",
  "wh.f.sm": "The Space Marines (Adeptus Astartes) are the genetically enhanced superhuman warriors of the Imperium of Man and its most famous elite force. Each Marine receives organs grown from the gene-seed of his Chapter's primarch and fights in power armour. Chapters number around a thousand warriors and most follow the Codex Astartes. Revered as the Angels of Death and humanity's unbreakable defenders, they are nonetheless distant from ordinary people and often inspire fear.",
  "wh.f.sob": "The Sisters of Battle (Adepta Sororitas) are the militant orders of the Ecclesiarchy, the Imperial church that worships the God-Emperor. They are ordinary humans without genetic enhancement, yet their unshakeable faith is said to work miracles on the battlefield. Armed with bolters, flamers and meltas, they are merciless towards heretics, mutants and witches. They embody the Imperium's religious fervour, at once inspiring and terrifying.",
  "wh.f.tau": "The T'au are a young, rapidly advancing race building the T'au Empire under the banner of the Greater Good. Their society is divided into castes: Fire, Earth, Air, Water and the Ethereals, who rule the rest. They rely on advanced technology, battlesuits, long-ranged weapons and drones, preferring firepower to close combat. They readily absorb other species into their empire, though many see totalitarian control behind the idealism of the Greater Good.",
  "wh.f.tyranids": "The Tyranids are an alien swarm that came from beyond the galaxy. Their hive fleets devour entire planets, breaking down all organic matter into biomass for new creatures. Every organism in the swarm, from tiny gaunts to towering Hive Tyrants, is linked to the Hive Mind, a collective consciousness that casts a terrifying Shadow in the Warp. Tyranids adapt swiftly to any foe and know neither fear nor negotiation.",
  "wh.waaagh": "The WAAAGH! is at once the Orks' battle cry, their great crusade of conquest and the psychic energy they give off. When a particularly strong and lucky warboss rises, mobs flock to him from everywhere and the horde begins to roll across the galaxy like an avalanche. The more Orks gather and the more excited they get, the stronger the WAAAGH! field, which powers their weirdboyz and even keeps their ramshackle machines working. A great WAAAGH! can sweep away entire sectors of the Imperium.",
  "wi.g.aard": "Aard is the witcher Sign of telekinesis, releasing a powerful wave of force. It knocks back and stuns enemies, knocks them off their feet, and can smash fragile walls and obstacles or put out fires. It is one of the simplest and most versatile Signs, useful in combat and exploration alike. In The Witcher 3 an upgraded Aard can strike all around the witcher.",
  "wi.g.axii": "Axii is the witcher Sign of suggestion, which acts on the mind. In combat it briefly stuns an enemy or even makes it fight on the witcher's side. Outside combat it helps persuade others, opening new dialogue options. It is a subtle Sign of control rather than force, and works less well against strong wills.",
  "wi.g.igni": "Igni is the witcher Sign of fire, releasing a jet or burst of flame. It sets enemies alight, making them burn and panic, and is especially effective against creatures that fear fire. Igni also lights torches and braziers and ignites explosive gases. It is the most destructive of the basic Signs, though dangerous for a careless witcher.",
  "wi.g.quen": "Quen is the witcher's protective Sign, surrounding the witcher with a magical shield. The shield absorbs blows, and in its stronger forms it can reflect damage back at attackers or restore health. It is one of the most reliable Signs for surviving combat, especially against powerful foes. Many Witcher 3 players consider it indispensable.",
  "wi.g.yrden": "Yrden is the witcher Sign of the trap: a magical glyph inscribed on the ground. It slows enemies who step into its circle and robs wraiths and other incorporeal creatures of their ability to vanish, leaving them vulnerable. Yrden takes preparation but is indispensable against spectres and in defence. In The Witcher 3 its alternate form strikes enemies with magical bolts.",
  "wi.s.bear": "The School of the Bear is a witcher school whose stronghold, Haern Caduch, is hidden in the Amell Mountains. Bears were known for being solitary and self-reliant, often working alone and treating monster hunting as a trade. Their style relied on strength, endurance and heavy armour. In The Witcher 3, Bear gear is the heaviest of the witcher sets.",
  "wi.s.cat": "The School of the Cat is the witcher school with the worst reputation. Cat witchers are thought to be unstable, perhaps due to altered training or mutations, and many work as assassins and mercenaries, willing to take contracts on people. They rely on speed, agility and light armour. The school seems to have no lasting stronghold, and its members are scattered across the world. In the games, Cat gear rewards fast attacks.",
  "wi.s.griffin": "The School of the Griffin is a witcher school founded by Erland of Larvik, with its stronghold Kaer Seren on the coast of Kovir and Poviss. Griffins aimed to preserve a knightly image of the witcher as a servant of the people, and their keep was famed for its library. They emphasised Signs and magic more than other schools. Like the rest, the school suffered from persecution of witchers. In The Witcher 3 its gear suits a Sign-focused build.",
  "wi.s.manticore": "The School of the Manticore is the most mysterious of the witcher schools, operating far to the east, beyond the Northern Kingdoms and Nilfgaard. Its history and style are described in more detail in The Witcher tabletop roleplaying game, which links it to lands across the Korath Desert. Manticore witchers are known for their skill in alchemy and poisons. In The Witcher 3 their gear is built for an alchemy-focused playstyle.",
  "wi.s.viper": "The School of the Viper is a witcher school whose stronghold, Gorthur Gvaed, lies in the Tir Tochair mountains in the south, in lands later part of Nilfgaard. Its most famous member is Letho of Gulet, the kingslayer of The Witcher 2. After the school declined, its last members served Nilfgaard as assassins. Vipers were known for their cold composure, poisons and lethal efficiency.",
  "wi.s.wolf": "The School of the Wolf is the best-known witcher school, based in the old keep of Kaer Morhen in the mountains of Kaedwen. Its graduates include Geralt of Rivia, Vesemir, Eskel and Lambert. Wolf witchers use a balanced style combining swordsmanship, Signs and alchemy. The keep suffered an attack by fanatics, and by Geralt's time the school had all but faded. Its last members keep to the old code and try to remain neutral.",
  "wod.aus.ahroun": "An Ahroun is a Garou born under the full moon. They are born warriors, first into battle against the Wyrm and defenders of their sept, with the highest Rage of any auspice. Ahroun are brave and indomitable, but their fury can endanger their own and their lives are often short. They are valued for their courage and strength, though their temper is feared.",
  "wod.aus.galliard": "A Galliard is a Garou born under the gibbous moon. They are bards, storytellers and keepers of memory who sing of great deeds, pass on legends and rouse their kin before battle. Galliards are passionate, emotional and often the heart of their sept. Their songs and tales preserve the history of the Garou Nation for future generations.",
  "wod.aus.philodox": "A Philodox is a Garou born under the half moon, the symbol of balance. They are judges and lawkeepers, guardians of the Litany who settle disputes, oversee fair challenges and pass sentence. A Philodox strives to see both sides of every question and judge impartially, even when it is unpopular. They are respected for their wisdom, though sometimes thought too stern.",
  "wod.aus.ragabash": "A Ragabash is a Garou born under the new moon, when the moon is hidden. They are tricksters, scouts and jesters whose role is to ask awkward questions and test traditions. They may break rules others would never be forgiven for, since this exposes weaknesses in Garou society. Ragabash start with the lowest Rage of any auspice.",
  "wod.aus.theurge": "A Theurge is a Garou born under the crescent moon. They are shamans and mystics who mediate between the Garou and the spirit world, bargaining with spirits, crafting fetishes and leading rites. Theurges often have prophetic dreams, and others find them a little detached from the world. Their Rage is modest but their Gnosis is strong.",
  "wod.banality": "Banality is the force of disbelief, boredom and cold rationalism, the opposite of Glamour and deadly to the fae. Rated from 1 to 10, it shows how deeply a changeling is touched by the mundane: the higher it is, the more their fae nature is suppressed and their magic weakened. High Banality risks the Undoing, in which a changeling forgets their true self entirely. Banality also serves as a defense against others' enchantments.",
  "wod.breed.homid": "A Homid is a Garou born in human form to a human parent. They grow up as ordinary people and often learn their true nature only at their First Change, which can be deeply shocking. Homids understand the human world and its technology well, but their bond with the spirits is weaker than that of wolf-born Garou. Today they are the most numerous breed.",
  "wod.breed.lupus": "A Lupus is a Garou born as a wolf to a wolf parent. They grow up in a pack, think instinctively and sense nature and the spirit world more keenly than others, so their Gnosis starts high. The human world, with its machines and laws, is strange and confusing to them. As wild wolves dwindle, fewer Lupus are born, and many blame humanity for it.",
  "wod.breed.metis": "A Metis, child of a forbidden bond, is born from the union of two Garou in breach of the Litany. They are born in the war-form Crinos, are sterile and each bears an inborn deformity or flaw. Garou society often treats them as a shame and keeps them at a distance, yet many grow tough and wise from fighting for their place since childhood. Unlike other breeds, they are raised among werewolves and know their nature from birth.",
  "wod.court.seelie": "The Seelie Court is one of the two great political and philosophical factions of fae society. Its followers honor tradition, chivalry, keeping one's word and protecting the weak, seeing these as the foundation of order. By custom the Seelie Court rules during the bright half of the year, from Beltaine to Samhain. Most noble sidhe houses are Seelie, though commoners of every kith belong to the court as well.",
  "wod.court.unseelie": "The Unseelie Court is the second great faction of fae society, defined in opposition to the Seelie. The Unseelie prize change, freedom, passion and honesty to oneself over rigid custom. They are not necessarily evil; to them chaos is the engine that keeps the Dreaming renewed. By tradition the Unseelie Court rules during the dark half of the year, from Samhain to Beltaine.",
  "wod.demon.a.defilers": "Lammasu is the original angelic name of the House of Defilers, the Fifth House. These angels ruled the seas, rivers and depths, as well as the hidden currents of emotion and longing. They awakened in humans a love of beauty, a yearning for the unattainable and the drive to create. The name recalls their bond with water and inspiration, while the mortal name reflects how the Fallen learned to exploit desire.",
  "wod.demon.a.devils": "Namaru is the original angelic name of the House of Devils, the First House, given to them at the Creation. The Namaru were the Creator's heralds, carrying His word and will to the angelic host and all of creation, and their voices and radiance inspired awe. The name recalls that the Devils once stood closest to Heaven's throne. Many of the Fallen still prefer it to the name mortals gave them.",
  "wod.demon.a.devourers": "Rabisu is the original angelic name of the House of Devourers, the Sixth House. Its angels cared for beasts, plants and living flesh, instilling primal vitality and instinct in the world. They were shepherds of everything that grows, runs and hunts. The mortal name Devourers reflects the ferocity and hunger that came to dominate the house after the Fall.",
  "wod.demon.a.fiends": "Neberu is the original angelic name of the House of Fiends, the Fourth House. These angels were keepers of the heavenly bodies who charted and read destiny in the stars, seeing the patterns that bind past and future. The name reflects their gift of foresight and their role as interpreters of the Creator's design. After the rebellion they revealed these secrets to humanity.",
  "wod.demon.a.malefactors": "Annunaki is the original angelic name of the House of Malefactors, the Third House. The Annunaki were angels of the earth and the artisans of Creation who raised mountains, carved riverbeds and filled the deep places with metal and stone. The name emphasizes their role as makers and craftsmen rather than destroyers. The house earned its mortal name from the weapons and artifacts it forged for the war against Heaven.",
  "wod.demon.a.scourges": "Asharu is the original angelic name of the House of Scourges, the Second House. These angels were guardians of the firmament and the breath of life who watched over living creatures, brought the winds and healed creation. The name reflects their first purpose as protectors and healers. The name Scourges later attached to the house because of the plagues and calamities some of them unleashed after the Fall.",
  "wod.demon.a.slayers": "Halaku is the original angelic name of the House of Slayers, the Seventh House. These were the angels of death who gently parted souls from bodies at life's end and guided them onward, upholding the natural cycle. The name stresses that for them death was a sacred duty rather than murder. The mortal name Slayers arose only after the Fall, when death became a weapon in their hands.",
  "wod.demon.defilers": "Defilers are the Fifth House of the Fallen, the angels of the seas, the depths and the waters, bound to emotion, dreams and yearning. They once inspired humanity toward art and the love of beauty, and after the Fall they learned to awaken and exploit desire. Defilers are as changeable as water, charming and inventive. Their Lores are the Lore of Longing, the Lore of Storms and the Lore of Transfiguration.",
  "wod.demon.devils": "Devils are the First House of the Fallen, once the heralds of Heaven who proclaimed the Creator's will to all of creation. Lucifer himself came from their ranks and led the rebellion, and many Devils served as its generals and ideologues. They are born leaders and orators, able to inspire awe and sway crowds. Their Lores are the Lore of the Celestials, the Lore of Flame and the Lore of Radiance.",
  "wod.demon.devourers": "Devourers are the Sixth House of the Fallen, the angels of the wild who tended beasts, plants and the flesh of every living creature. They nurtured the primal vitality of life and taught humans to hunt and survive. After the Fall the Devourers came to embody the untamed fury and hunger of the wilderness. Their Lores are the Lore of the Beast, the Lore of Flesh and the Lore of the Wild.",
  "wod.demon.fiends": "Fiends are the Fourth House of the Fallen, the angels of the heavenly bodies who read and charted the paths of destiny in the stars. They perceived the patterns that connect all events and shared knowledge of the future with humanity. After the rebellion Fiends became seers and masters of fate and of mystical gateways. Their Lores are the Lore of Light, the Lore of Patterns and the Lore of Portals.",
  "wod.demon.malefactors": "Malefactors are the Third House of the Fallen, the angels of the earth who shaped mountains, rivers and everything within the ground. They were the greatest artisans of Creation, and after the rebellion they forged weapons and artifacts for the war against Heaven. Malefactors are practical, patient and value what is made by hand. Their Lores are the Lore of the Earth, the Lore of the Forge and the Lore of Paths.",
  "wod.demon.scourges": "Scourges are the Second House of the Fallen, the angels of the firmament and the breath of life who protected living things and stirred the winds. They breathed life into the Creator's works and healed them. After the rebellion some Scourges turned to spreading disease and plague, which gave the house its name. Their Lores are the Lore of Awakening, the Lore of the Firmament and the Lore of the Winds.",
  "wod.demon.slayers": "Slayers are the Seventh House of the Fallen, the angels of death who guided souls onward and oversaw the natural end of every life. Before the rebellion death was merely a transition, and they carried out that duty with care. After the Fall the Slayers became masters of decay, of ghosts and of the boundary between worlds. Their Lores are the Lore of Death, the Lore of the Realms and the Lore of the Spirit.",
  "wod.fac.cryptic": "Cryptics are the faction of the Fallen who seek to understand the true reasons behind everything that has happened. They ask about the Creator's plan, why He allowed the rebellion, and what became of the world during their millennia of imprisonment. Cryptics gather knowledge, ancient texts and secrets, believing the answers will reveal the way forward. It is among the most enigmatic of the factions.",
  "wod.fac.faustian": "Faustians are the faction of the Fallen who see humanity as the key to victory and salvation. They gather mortal Faith through pacts and thralls, building networks of followers and influence. Some Faustians treat humans as nothing more than a resource, while others genuinely want to elevate them and restore their lost divine spark. It is a notably pragmatic faction.",
  "wod.fac.luciferan": "Luciferans are the faction of the Fallen who remain loyal to Lucifer and carry on his cause. They believe the Morningstar will return and are preparing an army to resume the war against Heaven. Luciferans value discipline, hierarchy and the old martial traditions of the rebellion. Their goal is victory in the great conflict begun before the dawn of time.",
  "wod.fac.ravener": "Raveners are the faction of the Fallen who have concluded that their damnation is eternal and there will be no redemption. If they cannot return to the Creator, they mean to destroy Creation itself, taking revenge on God and ending their own suffering. Raveners are the most dangerous and nihilistic group, feared even by other demons. Many of them are deeply consumed by Torment.",
  "wod.fac.reconciler": "Reconcilers are the faction of the Fallen who seek to undo the harm the war and the Fall inflicted on the world. They want to restore the Earth to its original beauty and become again the caring stewards of Creation they once were. Some hope for reconciliation with Heaven, others merely for atonement toward humanity and nature. Reconcilers lean toward building and mercy, though their methods are not always gentle.",
  "wod.faith": "Faith is the measure of a demon's spiritual power, drawn from the belief and devotion of mortals. Rated from 1 to 10, it shows how large a reserve of this power the demon holds and how potent their evocations can be. Faith is spent to empower Lores, assume the apocalyptic form and seal pacts, and is regained through worship, prayer and pacts with mortals. High Faith makes one of the Fallen formidable, but also draws the attention of rivals.",
  "wod.fey.boggan": "Boggans are hardworking household fae, short and sturdy with clever hands. They take genuine pleasure in honest labor such as cleaning, cooking and craftwork, and they are inveterate gossips who always know the local news. Boggans gladly help the humble and the needy but have little patience for arrogance and idleness. Their weakness is a compulsion to finish any task they start and to aid those in real need.",
  "wod.fey.clurichaun": "Clurichauns are kin to the leprechauns of Irish folklore: jovial brawlers fond of drink, song and a good fight. They are charming, lucky and generous to friends, yet quick to take offense. Clurichauns are known for their love of mischief and a knack for finding what others have hidden. Their weakness is a hot temper: once their pride is stung, they can hardly keep themselves from a quarrel or a scuffle.",
  "wod.fey.eshu": "The Eshu are fae wanderers and storytellers whose roots lie in the myths of Africa, India and the Near East. They roam endlessly in search of adventures and tales, and their gift lets them find their way to wherever something important is happening. Eshu are superb raconteurs and pathfinders who hold hospitality sacred. Their weakness is recklessness: they find it almost impossible to refuse a risky challenge or a dare.",
  "wod.fey.ghille": "The Ghille Dhu are fae of forests and growing things, drawn from Scottish and Irish tales of tree spirits. Their chimerical forms resemble living greenery, with bark-like skin and hair of leaves or moss. They guard wild groves and are deeply attuned to the turning of the seasons, which shapes both their looks and their moods. Cities of stone and asphalt make them uneasy, so Ghille Dhu seldom stray far from nature for long.",
  "wod.fey.nocker": "Nockers are cantankerous geniuses of craft and invention, able to build almost any device, including chimerical ones. They are notorious for foul tempers and an endless, colorful stream of curses while they work. Their curse is that nothing they make is ever perfect: every creation carries some hidden flaw, and they know it. For all their grumbling, a good nocker is an invaluable ally to any changeling.",
  "wod.fey.pooka": "Pooka are playful shapeshifting fae, each tied to a particular animal such as a cat, fox, raven or hare. They can take the form of that animal and share many of its habits. Pooka are incorrigible pranksters, and their defining weakness is that they can hardly ever state the truth plainly: they lie, embellish or answer in riddles instead. Their friends learn that genuine warmth often hides behind the tall tales.",
  "wod.fey.redcap": "Redcaps are crude and dangerous fae, famous for being able and eager to bite through and eat almost anything, from food to metal. Their looks and manners frighten others, and they happily use that fear to bully and assert themselves. Redcaps respect strength and fearlessness, and nobles often keep them as enforcers. Their weakness is their reputation: other fae treat them with suspicion and distaste.",
  "wod.fey.satyr": "Satyrs are passionate fae with goat legs and horns, embodying wild nature, music and celebration. They live intensely and impulsively, cherishing wine, dance, art and long philosophical conversations. Satyrs are known for their stamina and for kindling joy and inspiration in those around them. Their weakness is a struggle to restrain their impulses: once swept up in the moment, they easily forget duty and caution.",
  "wod.fey.selkie": "Selkies are fae of the sea who can transform into seals by means of a magical sealskin coat. They dwell along coastlines, love the ocean and its creatures, and often act as its defenders against pollution and hunters. The coat is both the source of their power and their great vulnerability: whoever steals it gains a hold over the selkie. A selkie who loses the coat forever loses their bond with the sea.",
  "wod.fey.sidhe": "The Sidhe are the noble fae, the aristocracy of the Dreaming, marked by unearthly beauty and regal bearing. Most of them returned from Arcadia only in 1969, the event called the Resurgence, and reclaimed rulership, which led to the Accordance War with the commoner kith. Sidhe excel at etiquette, intrigue and governance. Their weakness is an acute vulnerability to Banality, which wounds them more deeply than other kith.",
  "wod.fey.sluagh": "The Sluagh are pale, gaunt and quiet fae, collectors of secrets and students of everything forgotten or morbid. They haunt attics, cellars and abandoned houses, can squeeze through impossibly narrow gaps and possess uncannily sharp senses. Sluagh can speak only in a whisper; raising their voices is almost beyond them. Other changelings find them unsettling but value them as gatherers of hidden knowledge and go-betweens with the world of ghosts.",
  "wod.fey.troll": "Trolls are towering, mighty fae famed for their strength, endurance and loyalty. They serve as bodyguards, sentinels and warriors, and their word is as solid as stone. Trolls value honor and duty above all and rarely break an oath. Their weakness follows from this: a troll who betrays an obligation or a liege loses their supernatural strength until the wrong is set right.",
  "wod.gen": "Generation shows how many steps separate a vampire from Caine, the first of the Kindred: Caine is the first generation, and each Embrace adds one to the sire's generation. The lower the generation, the more potent the blood: such a vampire holds and spends more blood, can raise traits beyond normal limits and resists mental domination better. Most modern neonates are of the 12th or 13th generation, while ancient elders may be 4th to 6th. A vampire can lower their generation through diablerie, devouring the soul of an elder.",
  "wod.glamour": "Glamour is the energy of dreams, inspiration and creativity, the very essence of the fae and the fuel of their magic. The rating shows how much of this power a changeling can hold, on a scale of 1 to 10, with a high score meaning a strong bond to the Dreaming. Glamour is spent on cantrips (using the Arts) and sustaining chimera, and is regained through art, dreams, human inspiration and visits to enchanted places. Without Glamour the fae fade under the weight of the mundane.",
  "wod.gnosis": "Gnosis is the spiritual power of the Garou and the measure of their bond with Gaia and the spirit world. It usually ranges from 1 to 10 and is used to step sideways into the Umbra, activate fetishes, power some Gifts and deal with spirits. Rage and Gnosis pull against each other: in fury a werewolf loses touch with the spirits. Starting Gnosis depends on breed, highest for Lupus and lowest for Homids.",
  "wod.house.aesin": "House Aesin is a sidhe house of Norse and Celtic roots, born from the union of the warlike Aesir and the peaceful Vanir. Most of its members are Unseelie, though some are Seelie. The Aesin see themselves as guardians of the world against the threat of the Jotunn and keep a strict hierarchy and their Nine Virtues. They can speak with forest beasts, but they treat everyone except nobles as servants, which hampers their dealings with others.",
  "wod.house.ailil": "House Ailil is an Unseelie sidhe house known for its ambition, cunning and mastery of court politics. Its members treat intrigue as the natural order of things and seek power by any means, deftly manipulating allies and rivals alike. Ailil are proud, clever and dangerous in any debate. Many regard them as the natural leaders of the Unseelie Court, though other houses keep a wary eye on them.",
  "wod.house.balor": "House Balor is an Unseelie sidhe house descended from a union of the Fomorians and the Tuatha De Danann. It is organized like an army and considers itself permanently at war, preparing for the Evernight when the Unseelie will seize rule. Their Fomorian blood grants resistance to cold iron. In exchange, every member bears a permanent deformity of body, mind or emotion.",
  "wod.house.beaumayn": "House Beaumayn is a Seelie sidhe house known as the house of seers. Founded by the prophetic knight Jalendrel, it returned to Earth only with the Second Resurgence after a long confinement in Arcadia. Its members are natural prophets who receive visions in dreams and meditation, yet their warnings often go unheeded. The house carries the stain of an old heresy, and creatures of the Shadow Court can sense Beaumayn, making them targets.",
  "wod.house.daireann": "House Daireann is a sidhe house of Celtic roots, loosely aligned with the Unseelie Court yet blending traits of both courts. In the Mythic Age its members served as healers, warriors and champions, and they have returned to Earth to stand against the Fomorians. Above all, Daireann honor three laws: honor, vengeance and hospitality. They are fearless in battle but cannot keep a secret and are prone to boasting of their deeds.",
  "wod.house.dougal": "House Dougal is a Seelie sidhe house famed for its artisans and smiths, who forge the finest arms and armor in the Dreaming. Its members are practical and industrious and, unlike many sidhe, respect commoner craftsmen. By tradition each member bears some physical imperfection recalling the injury of the house's founder. Dougal values deeds over words and loyalty over glamour and show.",
  "wod.house.eiluned": "House Eiluned is a Seelie sidhe house of secret-keepers and masters of magic. Its members are renowned for their skill in sorcery, their insight and their talent for working intrigue from the shadows. Though formally loyal to the Seelie Court, the house is distrusted by other sidhe because of its secrecy. Eiluned value knowledge as the highest form of power and share their secrets reluctantly.",
  "wod.house.fiona": "House Fiona is a Seelie sidhe house known for its passion, courage and romantic exploits. Its members are ardent warriors and adventurers who throw themselves into battle and love with equal fervor. Fiona are sincere, generous and loyal to friends, and commoners respect them for their openness. Their weakness is a reckless intensity that makes them forget caution.",
  "wod.house.gwydion": "House Gwydion is a noble Seelie house of the sidhe, regarded as the most prestigious and traditionally the source of the High Kings. Its members are born rulers, judges and commanders, proud and demanding of themselves and others. Gwydion places justice and honor above all. Their weakness is suspicion and temper: they find it hard to trust others, and an insult can provoke a furious outburst.",
  "wod.house.leanhaun": "House Leanhaun is an Unseelie sidhe house whose members act as muses to mortal artists, poets and musicians. They kindle a creative fire in their protégés but feed on the Glamour it produces, often leaving the inspired person drained. Leanhaun are graceful, melancholy and surrounded by an air of tragic beauty. Without a steady source of Glamour they quickly wither, so their lives are an endless search for new talent.",
  "wod.house.liam": "House Liam is an exiled sidhe house, once Seelie, cast out by the rest of the nobility for its closeness to humanity. According to legend, the Liam discovered the Changeling Way, the ritual that lets fae be born into mortal bodies and so survive the Shattering. Members sympathize with commoners and mortals and often act as their champions. Because of their exile, many sidhe look down on them.",
  "wod.house.scathach": "House Scathach is a sidhe house aligned with neither the Seelie nor the Unseelie Court. Its founder was Scathach, the legendary Irish warrior-woman who trained heroes. The house underwent the Changeling Way ritual and became partly mortal, so it weathered the Shattering better than others. Scathach are silent, deadly guardians on the fringes of fae society who protect all fae from ancient enemies; their grey garb earned them the name Gray Walkers.",
  "wod.house.varich": "House Varich is an Unseelie sidhe house from Russia, descended from the ancient vila. It left the mortal world when the old faith was displaced, spent centuries in Arcadia's endless winter and has only recently returned. Their founder's tragic betrayal in love made the house students of fate's patterns, and Varich perceive the hidden order in battle, politics and music. They cannot swear vows of love and must accept every challenge offered to them.",
  "wod.lore.awakening": "The Lore of Awakening belongs to the House of Scourges and reflects their original role as bringers and keepers of life. A demon can sense vitality, heal wounds and sickness, strengthen the body and drive out ailments. At its highest rank they can even restore life to someone recently dead. A demon consumed by Torment turns these powers to harm, inflicting disease and decay.",
  "wod.lore.beasts": "The Lore of the Beast belongs to the House of Devourers (Rabisu) and grants mastery over animals. A demon can understand and speak with beasts, call them to their aid, bend them to their will and see through their eyes. At its highest ranks they can fully possess animals or direct whole packs and swarms against their enemies.",
  "wod.lore.celestials": "The Lore of the Celestials belongs to the House of Devils (Namaru) and reflects their role as heaven's messengers. It lets a demon perceive the Faith of mortals and demons as a glow, send visions and messages to others, and bolster or obstruct the powers of fellow angels. At its highest ranks the demon can strike with a blast of heavenly fire and even seize control of another demon's evocation and alter its outcome.",
  "wod.lore.death": "The Lore of Death belongs to the House of Slayers (Halaku) and deals with decay, aging and the end of life. A demon can sense approaching death, accelerate rot and wear in objects, wither plants and age the living. At its highest ranks they can end a life with a touch or a glance.",
  "wod.lore.earth": "The Lore of the Earth belongs to the House of Malefactors (Annunaki) and grants command over soil, stone and metal. A demon can sense whatever happens in the ground around them, pass through rock, reshape it and set it in motion. At its highest ranks it can shake the earth and bring buildings down as if in an earthquake.",
  "wod.lore.firmament": "The Lore of the Firmament belongs to the House of Scourges and lets a demon see and hear through the air, as they once watched over the world from the heavens. A demon can sense people's presence from afar, locate those they know, and spy on distant places. At its highest ranks they become nearly all-seeing across a wide area.",
  "wod.lore.flame": "The Lore of Flame belongs to the House of Devils and grants mastery over fire and heat. A demon can sense and extinguish flames, control their intensity and direction, shield themselves from burning and eventually conjure fire from nothing. At higher ranks it becomes a fearsome weapon able to reduce foes and entire buildings to ash.",
  "wod.lore.flesh": "The Lore of Flesh belongs to the House of Devourers and lets a demon reshape living flesh. A demon can strengthen their own body, grow claws or armor, speed healing, and mend or maim others. At its highest ranks they can completely remold bodies into almost any form.",
  "wod.lore.forge": "The Lore of the Forge belongs to the House of Malefactors and expresses their genius as makers. A demon can grasp how any object works, control machines, strengthen or ruin items and imbue them with special properties. At its highest ranks they craft true artifacts and relics that hold part of their own power or even a bound spirit.",
  "wod.lore.light": "The Lore of Light belongs to the House of Fiends (Neberu) and grants command over light and darkness. A demon can brighten or dim illumination, blind foes, wrap themselves in shadow and shape illusory images out of light. At its highest ranks they weave complex, convincing illusions and can strike enemies with searing beams.",
  "wod.lore.longing": "The Lore of Longing belongs to the House of Defilers (Lammasu) and deals with emotion and dreams. A demon can read people's feelings and deepest desires, heighten or soothe emotions, inspire creativity and instill a yearning for the unattainable. At high ranks they can take complete control of someone's emotional state. A Torment-ridden demon twists desire into consuming obsession.",
  "wod.lore.paths": "The Lore of Paths belongs to the House of Malefactors and concerns roads, routes and the space between places. A demon always finds the right way, can hide a path from others, confound pursuers or bar their passage. At its highest ranks they bend distance itself, shortening a long journey or turning a short road into an endless maze.",
  "wod.lore.patterns": "The Lore of Patterns belongs to the House of Fiends and deals with fate, probability and foresight. A demon can discern the threads of destiny, predict how events will unfold, bestow good or ill fortune and nudge chance in a desired direction. At its highest ranks they can reweave a person's fate and change the course of their life.",
  "wod.lore.portals": "The Lore of Portals belongs to the House of Fiends and grants power over gates, doors and passages. A demon can seal or open any passage, sense where doorways lead and ward them against intruders. At its highest ranks they open portals between distant places and travel across vast distances in an instant.",
  "wod.lore.radiance": "The Lore of Radiance belongs to the House of Devils and works on hearts and minds through angelic majesty. A demon can give their voice irresistible authority, fill people with hope and courage, stir buried memories of the celestials and inspire loyalty. At its highest ranks it places a mark of favor or disfavor on a person's aura and lays bare their true nature. A demon deep in Torment instead spreads terror and despair.",
  "wod.lore.realms": "The Lore of the Realms belongs to the House of Slayers and deals with the barrier between the living world and the Shadowlands. A demon can gauge the strength of that barrier, step into the realm of the dead and travel swiftly through it, and become immaterial. At its highest ranks they reach across the veil without crossing and open temporary gateways that other demons can pass through.",
  "wod.lore.spirit": "The Lore of the Spirit belongs to the House of Slayers and grants power over ghosts and souls. A demon can see and hear the spirits of the dead, speak with them, summon them and bend them to their will. At its highest ranks they bind souls to objects or places and can even hold a spirit within its body, preventing it from departing.",
  "wod.lore.storms": "The Lore of Storms belongs to the House of Defilers and grants command over water and weather. A demon can control water, breathe beneath it, call up fog and rain, and at the highest ranks summon true tempests with lightning and floods. This Lore recalls that the Defilers once ruled the seas and the depths.",
  "wod.lore.transfiguration": "The Lore of Transfiguration belongs to the House of Defilers and deals with mutable appearance. A demon can alter their own looks to seem more appealing or to resemble another person, and can sense how others wish to see them. At its highest ranks they assume someone else's form so completely that the deception is nearly impossible to detect.",
  "wod.lore.wild": "The Lore of the Wild belongs to the House of Devourers and grants command over plants and the wilderness. A demon can speed or halt plant growth, command roots and vines, vanish into the woods and make vegetation attack. At its highest ranks they awaken whole thickets and turn an area into an impassable tangle.",
  "wod.lore.winds": "The Lore of the Winds belongs to the House of Scourges (Asharu) and grants command over air and its motion. A demon can summon gusts, muffle or carry sound, bear scents and voices, and deflect objects flying toward them. At its highest ranks they raise hurricane-force whirlwinds capable of sweeping people away and tearing structures apart.",
  "wod.path.accord": "The Path of Honorable Accord is a chivalric code whose followers are called Knights. It centres on loyalty, keeping one's word and duty to sect and pack; breaking an oath is worse than death for them. The path dates back to the Middle Ages and survives mainly in the Sabbat. Its adherents are disciplined and reliable but demand the same of others and do not forgive betrayal.",
  "wod.path.blood": "The Path of Blood is the religious doctrine of the Assamites, built around reverence for their founder Haqim. Its followers believe the childer of Caine are accursed and must be converted or destroyed, and they seek to draw closer to their founder by lowering their generation. The path demands strict discipline, self-sacrifice and mercilessness toward the unworthy. Its adherents are zealous and dangerous holy warriors.",
  "wod.path.caine": "The Path of Caine is the creed of the Noddists, who study the Book of Nod and other legends of the first vampire. They believe a vampire should not cling to Humanity but strive to become like Caine, cultivating the power of the blood, wisdom and self-mastery. Its adherents are scholars, mystics and preachers of the Sabbat, known for asceticism and cold reason. Many scour ancient texts for signs of approaching Gehenna.",
  "wod.path.cathari": "The Path of Cathari descends from the medieval Albigensian heresy. Its followers believe flesh and the material world are corrupt, and that vampires are creatures of evil meant to serve as tempters. Instead of suppressing their desires they indulge in excess and lead others into temptation to test their souls. Found mostly in the Sabbat, the path does not demand pointless cruelty, only an honest embrace of one's dark nature.",
  "wod.path.feral": "The Path of the Feral Heart is the morality of those who accept the Beast as a natural part of themselves; its followers are called Beasts. They live as predators, hunting to feed rather than to be cruel, avoiding overthinking and respecting the laws of nature. It is most common among the Gangrel. Its adherents are blunt, dislike civilisation and feel at home in the wild.",
  "wod.path.humanity": "Humanity measures how much of their mortal self a vampire retains and how well they restrain the Beast within. It runs from 10 down to 0: each cruel or inhuman act risks losing a point, and as Humanity falls the vampire grows more alien to mortals and more prone to frenzy. At zero the Beast consumes the personality entirely. Most Kindred, especially in the Camarilla, hold to this morality.",
  "wod.path.lilith": "The Path of Lilith is the secretive creed of the Bahari, who revere Lilith, Adam's first wife, as the true dark mother of vampires and Caine's teacher. They believe enlightenment comes through pain and suffering, which teaches and transforms. The Bahari see Caine as an ungrateful pupil and the Book of Nod as a distorted account. Followers are rare, keep to the shadows and are distrusted by other Kindred.",
  "wod.path.metamorph": "The Path of Metamorphosis is a Tzimisce doctrine that treats vampirism as merely a stage toward something higher. Its followers, the Metamorphosists, seek to transcend both human and vampiric existence by studying and experimenting with flesh through Vicissitude. To that end they shed emotions and attachments. They are reclusive and cold, regarded as some of the most alien beings in the Sabbat.",
  "wod.path.night": "The Path of Night is a grim doctrine found mainly among the Lasombra; its followers are called Nihilists. They believe vampires are damned by God and meant to serve as instruments of darkness and sin, driving mortals to fear and repentance. They seek no salvation, yet avoid mindless brutality: the terror they spread is meant to teach a lesson.",
  "wod.path.paradox": "The Path of Paradox is the morality of the Ravnos clan, rooted in Indian ideas of karma and rebirth. Its followers, the Shilmulo, believe vampires have fallen out of the wheel of samsara and that each must follow their own destined duty, svadharma. One expression of this is destroying other vampires to return them to the cycle of rebirth. The path nearly vanished along with most of the clan after the catastrophic Week of Nightmares.",
  "wod.path.power": "The Path of Power and the Inner Voice is the morality of the Sabbat's born leaders, known as Unifiers. They hold that power is a vampire's natural goal and that one should heed only one's own will. A follower must rise on merit, use subordinates according to their abilities and reward success, while accepting personal responsibility for failure. Many Sabbat bishops and archbishops follow it.",
  "wod.path.scorched": "The Path of the Scorched Heart is an austere creed found mostly among the True Brujah; its adherents are called the Unforgiving. They believe every emotion springs from the Beast and leads to corruption, so they strive to eradicate feeling entirely and act on pure logic. They see morality as emotion-based and therefore unreliable, valuing only facts, knowledge and calculation. The path is associated with the secret sect known as the Tal'Mahe'Ra.",
  "wod.path.self": "The Path of Self-Focus draws heavily on Eastern philosophies; its adherents are called Internalists. They hold that the Beast is calmed not by fighting the world but by acceptance and non-interference: one should change oneself rather than circumstances. Internalists live in the present moment, pursue deep self-knowledge and consider themselves answerable only to themselves. The path is associated with the secret sect known as the Tal'Mahe'Ra.",
  "wod.path.typhon": "The Path of Typhon is the religious morality of the Followers of Set, whose adherents are called Theophidians. They believe the soul is shackled by illusions and taboos that can only be broken through vice and temptation. The path's ultimate aim is to prepare for Set's resurrection and serve him. Its followers are patient, calculating and keenly aware of the weaknesses of those around them.",
  "wod.rage": "Rage is the primal fury of the Garou, Gaia's wrath running through their veins. It usually ranges from 1 to 10: the higher it is, the stronger and more dangerous the werewolf. Rage lets a Garou act with inhuman speed but risks frenzy, in which they lose control of themselves. High Rage also unsettles humans and animals, isolating the werewolf from ordinary mortals. A Garou's starting Rage depends on their auspice.",
  "wod.rank.adren": "Adren is the third, middle rank of the Garou. Its holders are seasoned werewolves known within and beyond their sept, often leading packs and mentoring the young. Few reach Adren, since it requires renown gathered over years of dangerous living. Their opinions carry weight at councils.",
  "wod.rank.athro": "Athro is the fourth rank of the Garou, held by celebrated heroes and sages. An Athro often holds an important office in the sept, leads rites or commands the defence of a caern. Their names are known across many septs and their word carries great weight. It is the step that leads to the highest rank, Elder.",
  "wod.rank.cliath": "Cliath is the first rank of the Garou, earned by a young werewolf after the Rite of Passage. A Cliath is recognised as a full member of the tribe but must still earn Glory, Honor and Wisdom to rise further. They are expected to defer to their elders and keep learning, and are often eager to prove themselves.",
  "wod.rank.elder": "Elder is the fifth and highest ordinary rank of the Garou. It is granted only to living legends whose deeds are sung by Galliards throughout the Nation. Elders lead septs and tribes, keep ancient wisdom and decide the fates of their kind. Few Garou live long enough to reach it, and even rivals heed their words.",
  "wod.rank.fostern": "Fostern is the second rank of the Garou, showing that a werewolf has proven themselves and earned their sept's recognition. Fosterns have experience in battles and rites, are trusted with important tasks and often lead smaller undertakings for their pack. It is the rank of those who are no longer novices but not yet established leaders.",
  "wod.sect.cam": "The Camarilla is the largest sect of vampires, founded in the 15th century to survive the Inquisition and the Anarch revolt. Its central law is the Masquerade: mortals must never learn that vampires exist. Cities are ruled by Princes upholding the Traditions with councils of elders, and the sect is led by its Inner Circle. It claims to welcome all Kindred, but its core consists of the Ventrue, Toreador, Tremere, Brujah, Malkavian and Nosferatu clans.",
  "wod.sect.sab": "The Sabbat is a militant sect that rejects Humanity and the rule of the ancient Antediluvians. Its members see themselves as predators rather than people and prepare for Gehenna, the final war against the clan founders. Sabbat vampires are organised in packs bound by the Vaulderie ritual, which replaces individual blood bonds with communal loyalty. Its leading clans are the Lasombra and Tzimisce, joined by antitribu who broke from other clans. Many members follow Paths of Enlightenment.",
  "wod.seem.grump": "Grump is the seeming of a changeling whose mortal body has reached mature adulthood. Grumps have accumulated knowledge, experience and wisdom and often serve as mentors and advisers. With the years, however, Banality weighs on them more heavily, and holding on to the Dreaming grows harder. Behind many a grump's grumbling lies deep care for the young and worry for the future of the fae.",
  "wod.seem.wilder": "Wilder is the seeming of a young adult changeling whose fae soul still burns brightly. Wilders are full of energy, passion and rebellion, eager to experience everything and to test themselves. They have the freedom of adults yet have not lost their bond with the Dreaming. Wilders make up most adventuring changelings, though their impetuousness often leads to trouble.",
  "wod.vamp.assamite": "The Assamites are an independent clan based in the fortress of Alamut in the Middle East, devoted to their founder Haqim. They are best known as peerless assassins who take payment in vampiric blood, though the clan also includes castes of scholars (viziers) and sorcerers. Their Disciplines are Celerity, Obfuscate and Quietus, the art of lethal blood. For centuries a Tremere curse kept them from drinking the blood of other Kindred.",
  "wod.vamp.brujah": "The Brujah are a clan of rebels and restless idealists. Once the philosopher-warriors of ancient Carthage, many are now street agitators, Anarchs and would-be revolutionaries. Their blood grants Celerity, Potence and Presence, along with a notorious temper that makes them quicker to frenzy than other Kindred. They belong to the Camarilla, but grudgingly, forever arguing with its elders.",
  "wod.vamp.gangrel": "The Gangrel are loners and wanderers, the clan closest to the Beast. They roam the wilderness, can sleep within the earth itself and are reputed to be the finest fighters among the Kindred. Their Disciplines are Animalism, Fortitude and Protean, which lets them grow claws or take the shape of a wolf or bat. Each frenzy leaves a lasting animal feature on their bodies. Around the turn of the millennium most of the clan left the Camarilla and went independent.",
  "wod.vamp.giovanni": "The Giovanni are an independent clan of necromancers who are also a wealthy Venetian family of bankers and merchants. They rose to power in the 15th century by destroying the Cappadocian clan and its founder. Their Disciplines are Necromancy, Dominate and Potence, and their bite causes their victims agonising pain. The clan keeps to itself under the Promise, a pact with the Camarilla to stay out of its affairs, and places family above everything.",
  "wod.vamp.lasombra": "The Lasombra are a proud clan of aristocrats and manipulators who command darkness. Their unique Discipline, Obtenebration, summons living shadows and tendrils of darkness, and they also wield Dominate and Potence. They cast no reflection in mirrors. Historically tied to the Church, nearly the entire clan serves the Sabbat, where its members hold many of the leading positions.",
  "wod.vamp.malkavian": "The Malkavians are a clan of madmen and seers. Every one suffers an incurable derangement, yet their madness often hides unsettling insight. Their Disciplines are Auspex, Dementation and Obfuscate, and their shared blood links them through a strange collective 'Cobweb'. Other Kindred both fear and underestimate them, since their pranks and prophecies have a way of coming true. They are members of the Camarilla.",
  "wod.vamp.nosferatu": "The Nosferatu are a clan whose Embrace twists each member into a hideous, monstrous form. Unable to walk openly among mortals, they hide in sewers and tunnels, building secret warrens beneath the cities. Necessity made them gatherers of rumours and secrets, and they are now the foremost information brokers among the Kindred. Their Disciplines are Animalism, Obfuscate and Potence. The clan is known for its loyalty to its own and belongs to the Camarilla.",
  "wod.vamp.setite": "The Followers of Set are an independent clan descended from the Egyptian dark god Set, whom they worship as a deity. Setites are tempters and dealers in vice: they find the weaknesses of mortals and Kindred and bind them with dependency, claiming this frees the soul. Their Disciplines are Obfuscate, Presence and Serpentis. Bright light is especially harmful to them. Almost every other clan regards them with suspicion.",
  "wod.vamp.toreador": "The Toreador are a clan of artists, connoisseurs and socialites. They surround themselves with beauty, patronise the arts and set the fashions of Kindred society, which leads other clans to dismiss them as shallow decadents. Their Disciplines are Auspex, Celerity and Presence. Their weakness is a tendency to become entranced by something truly beautiful and lose track of everything else. They are pillars of the Camarilla and staunch defenders of the Masquerade.",
  "wod.vamp.tremere": "The Tremere are a clan of blood sorcerers, descended from medieval mages who seized immortality through ritual and stolen vampiric blood. Their signature Discipline is Thaumaturgy, the magic of blood, alongside Auspex and Dominate. The clan is organised as a strict pyramid centred in Vienna, and its neonates are partially blood-bound to their elders. Their origins earn them widespread distrust, yet they remain a powerful and valued part of the Camarilla.",
  "wod.vamp.tzimisce": "The Tzimisce are an ancient clan of scholars and lords from Eastern Europe, above all the Carpathians. Their signature Discipline, Vicissitude, lets them sculpt flesh and bone like clay, their own or anyone else's, and they also practise Animalism and Auspex. They must rest in the soil of their homeland or grow weak. They are known for strict hospitality, meticulous intellect and a cold, alien detachment, and together with the Lasombra they form the core of the Sabbat.",
  "wod.vamp.ventrue": "The Ventrue are the clan of aristocrats, rulers and financiers, and see themselves as the backbone of the Camarilla. Many Princes come from their ranks, and they prize tradition, order and responsibility for their domains. Their Disciplines are Dominate, Fortitude and Presence. Their curse is a rarefied palate: each Ventrue can only feed on one particular type of mortal and cannot keep down any other blood.",
  "wod.wolf.fangs": "The Silver Fangs are the oldest royal tribe of the Garou and have long led the Nation. Their patron is Falcon, and they guard their bloodlines carefully for purity. Yet centuries of inbreeding have taken a toll: many Fangs suffer from madness and odd derangements. They are noble, proud and brave, but their right to rule is questioned more and more.",
  "wod.wolf.fenris": "The Get of Fenris are a warrior tribe with roots in the Germanic and Norse lands. They prize strength, courage and glory in battle and despise weakness; their way is relentless war against the Wyrm. Their patron is the great wolf Fenris. They are among the most fearsome fighters of the Nation, but their harshness, intolerance and dark history cause friction even with allies.",
  "wod.wolf.fianna": "The Fianna are a tribe of bards, warriors and storytellers from the Celtic lands of Ireland, Scotland and Wales. They keep the songs and legends of the Garou, love feasting, music and poetry, and their rage runs as strong as their joy. Their patron is Stag, and they have long-standing ties to the fae. The Fianna are passionate, loyal to friends and known as the finest Galliards of the Nation.",
  "wod.wolf.gaia": "The Children of Gaia are the peacemakers and healers of the Garou. They try to reconcile feuding tribes, seek allies among humans and believe the Apocalypse must be met with compassion as well as claws. Their patron is Unicorn. Others often see them as too soft, but they fight fiercely when protecting the weak. The tribe welcomes anyone who shares its ideals.",
  "wod.wolf.glass": "The Glass Walkers are the most urban tribe of the Garou, having embraced technology and the modern world. They live in the great cities, know computers, finance and the underworld, and deal with spirits of machines and electricity. Their patron is Cockroach. Many Garou suspect them of drifting toward the Weaver, but the Glass Walkers insist the city must be defended too.",
  "wod.wolf.gnawers": "The Bone Gnawers are a tribe of the homeless and outcast, living at the very bottom of the cities. They protect the poor, the drifters and everyone society has abandoned, and they know the slums, dumps and back alleys intimately. Their patron is Rat. Other tribes long looked down on them, but the Bone Gnawers take pride in their toughness, resourcefulness and ability to survive anywhere.",
  "wod.wolf.shadow": "The Shadow Lords are an ambitious tribe of politicians and schemers from Eastern Europe. They respect strength, believe the most capable should lead and openly covet the Silver Fangs' place. Their patron is Grandfather Thunder, the spirit of the storm. Other tribes distrust their methods but admit they are effective and ruthless against Gaia's enemies.",
  "wod.wolf.stargazers": "The Stargazers are a tribe of philosophers and mystics who seek inner balance through meditation, martial arts and watching the stars. They strive to master their Rage and understand the workings of fate, and their teachings owe much to Eastern traditions. Their patron is Chimera. After disputes with other tribes, the Stargazers for a time left the Garou Nation and aligned with the shapeshifters of Asia.",
  "wod.wolf.striders": "The Silent Striders are a tribe of wanderers, messengers and scouts originating in ancient Egypt. An old curse exiled them from their homeland, and ever since they have roamed the world restlessly, carrying news between septs. Their patron is Owl. The Striders are closely tied to the realm of the dead and ancestral ghosts, and other Garou find them mysterious and a little eerie.",
  "wod.wolf.talons": "The Red Talons are a tribe that accepts only those born as wolves. They blame humans for destroying the wild and believe the best way to save Gaia is a drastic reduction of humanity. Their patron is Griffin. The Talons are ferocious, have little understanding of the human world and shun it, and they get along poorly with other tribes.",
  "wod.wolf.uktena": "The Uktena are a tribe of mystics and keepers of secrets, rooted in the Native peoples of North America. They study dark lore, guard the places where ancient evil spirits are bound and sometimes take risks by turning the Wyrm's own weapons against it. Their patron is Uktena, the great horned water serpent. Other tribes respect their wisdom but distrust their fascination with forbidden knowledge.",
  "wod.wolf.wendigo": "The Wendigo are a stern warrior tribe from the Native peoples of North America's northern forests. They uphold ancestral traditions, fight both the Wyrm and the legacy of colonisation and distrust the tribes that came from Europe. Their patron is Wendigo, the spirit of hunger and the cold wind. They are hardy hunters at home in winter lands and fiercely loyal to their people.",
  "wr.mut": "In the Warhammer worlds mutations are regarded as 'gifts' of the Chaos gods, signs that a mortal has caught their eye. They may start as a horn, an extra eye or a patch of scales and grow into tentacles, claws and ever more monstrous changes. To some they are a blessing and a path to power, to others a curse. Whoever receives too many gifts risks losing mind and form entirely and becoming a Chaos Spawn, a shapeless, ever-shifting beast. At the tenth mutation, that is exactly what happens.",
  "wr.of.khorne": "Khorne, the Blood God, is the Chaos god of rage, war and slaughter. Enthroned on a mountain of skulls amid a sea of blood, he asks his followers only to shed blood, and does not care whose. He despises sorcery and honours only martial prowess and the clash of arms. His servants are berserkers and frenzied warriors, and his sacred number is eight. Of all the Chaos powers he is the most direct and the most feared on the battlefield.",
  "wr.of.nurgle": "Nurgle, known as Grandfather Nurgle, is the Chaos god of disease, decay, despair and stubborn endurance. Paradoxically he is jovial and caring, lavishing his 'gifts' of plague and rot upon mortals out of genuine affection. His followers are bloated and pox-ridden yet feel no pain and are terribly hard to kill. He offers freedom from the fear of death by embracing the endless cycle of decay and rebirth. His sacred number is seven.",
  "wr.of.rat": "The Horned Rat is the god of the Skaven in Warhammer Fantasy, often counted as a Chaos power in its own right alongside the four great gods. It is a deity of treachery, cunning, hunger and teeming fertility that promises the ratmen dominion over the world. The Skaven believe it will one day devour all of existence. Its will is interpreted by the Council of Thirteen and by its horned prophets, the Grey Seers. Its sacred number is thirteen.",
  "wr.of.slaanesh": "Slaanesh, the Prince of Pleasure, is the youngest of the great Chaos gods, embodying excess, pride, vanity and the obsessive pursuit of perfection. Its birth in the realm of the Aeldari was a catastrophe that destroyed their empire, and it has hungered for their souls ever since. Its followers chase ever more intense sensations until they lose all restraint. Slaanesh seduces with the promise of perfection and fulfilled desire, but service leads only to jaded ruin. Its sacred number is six.",
  "wr.of.tzeentch": "Tzeentch, the Changer of Ways, is the Chaos god of change, hope, ambition, sorcery and scheming. He weaves endless plots so convoluted that no one but he understands their true aim, and perhaps not even he. His followers are sorcerers, rebels and anyone who craves knowledge and power. His gifts are unpredictable, and a favoured servant's body may never stop changing. He is the eternal rival of Nurgle, as change opposes stagnation. His sacred number is nine."
});
